WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.450
We’ve lit the fuse on a population bomb
that will wipe out all of humanity. There

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:11.050
is no tomorrow. Mass starvation and genocidal
wars over dwindling finite resources -- the

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:17.240
long-prophesied Norse Ragnarok, the Abrahamist
Last Judgment, the Nuutungk Talongvaqa, the

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:22.470
Hopi Tribe’s “Last Day.” If you're an
attractive, charming doomsday showman, the

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:23.700
end is near.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:28.429
And if you're a nerd whose best work solved
the problem of airline overbooking, things

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:30.420
have never been better.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.790
Because Armageddon is not just mythological
-- it’s a battle of science, nature, human

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:44.660
behavior, and two men: one, a Goliath celebrated
with academic accolades and public popularity,

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.270
the other, a David relegated to relative obscurity.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:54.239
It’s the story of a bet that bound them
together eternally, a bet over a handful of

00:00:54.239 --> 00:01:00.789
natural resources that served as a proxy for
our destiny. The lessons we learned from it

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:08.500
have almost been entirely ignored because
they defy every instinct we have about how

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.440
to survive.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:18.119
The foundational question of their wager is
more relevant now than ever: are we paradoxically

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:21.619
killing ourselves by existing?

00:01:21.619 --> 00:01:30.380
And if the facts say everything you think
and feel is wrong, would you even care?

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:39.549
“The battle to feed all of humanity is over.
In the 1970s and 1980s hundreds of millions

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:45.990
of people will starve to death in spite of
any crash programs embarked upon now. At this

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:51.689
late date nothing can prevent a substantial
increase in the world death rate.”

00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:57.619
In 1968, Paul Ehrlich opened his doomsday
prediction book “The Population Bomb”

00:01:57.619 --> 00:02:04.009
with those three sentences. Ehrlich was, and
still is, an entomologist -- his particular

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:08.990
field of study is butterflies, and 4 years
before publishing a book that would change

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:15.970
the world, he popularized a theory of ‘coevolution’
-- the concept that natural selection doesn’t

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:21.920
happen in isolation, and that plants and insects
can have a reciprocal relationship affecting

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.660
both their evolutionary paths.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:30.750
He’d spent decades thinking about how the
world and all the matter and life within its

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:37.550
ecosystem worked -- minerals, water, plants,
animals, humans -- and he concluded that it

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:43.590
didn’t. Or, that it didn’t anymore. He
saw the end of the world as we know it, and

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:49.690
he not only knew why it would happen, but
he also knew how to stop it.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.599
When he was catching butterflies as a kid
in 1930’s New Jersey -- his collection grew

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:59.730
so large that it filled his bedroom and he
had to sleep in a different room -- he had

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:06.450
no idea that his life was on a collision course
with a boy born in the same year living just

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:12.840
a few miles away. Julian Simon’s interest
was ultimately in economics, and the clash

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:20.091
between Ehrlich and Simon was a decades-long
debate to decide the fate of humanity… and

00:03:20.091 --> 00:03:21.091
you.

00:03:21.091 --> 00:03:27.690
It’s a debate over morals, mankind, and
math -- and nearly 200 years before the public

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:34.270
wager between Paul Ehrlich and Julian Simon
became the most famous bet in history, it

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:41.260
started with a political economist whose students
affectionately referred to as “web-toe.”

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:48.440
Thomas Malthus focused on economics and demography.
In his 1798 “An Essay on the Principle of

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.170
Population,” Malthus wrote that:

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:55.410
“It has been said that the great question
is now at issue, whether man shall henceforth

00:03:55.410 --> 00:04:02.459
start forwards with accelerated velocity toward
illimitable, and hitherto unconceived improvement,

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:08.750
or be condemned to a perpetual oscillation
between happiness and misery, and after every

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:15.180
effort remain still at an immeasurable distance
from the wished-for goal.”

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.180
OKAY.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:21.459
His life span almost perfectly covered the
beginning and end of the Industrial revolution,

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:27.580
and he was at the center of it in Great Britain.
It was an unprecedented period of innovation

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:33.940
-- for the first time, machines operated by
a growing urban population wove textiles,

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:40.139
which were then traded throughout a global
market of raw materials and finished goods.

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:45.330
Improvements in mining and refining made iron
production soar, and after 100 years steam

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:51.240
power had finally matured. Life was getting
better… fast.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:57.330
But if you watched my video on Ignaz Semmelweis
and the disgusting state of medicine in the

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:04.820
early 1800’s, you know that it was also
quickly getting worse. Despite all these material

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:11.440
improvements and increases in overall wealth,
human suffering seemed more severe than at

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:17.210
any point in history. Poverty, disease, and
strife ravaged the lower classes of the England

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:22.460
that surrounded Malthus -- and the environment
was being degraded, too.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:27.360
The River Thames had been the lifeblood of
the Southeast of England since before Julius

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:33.710
Caesar’s invasion -- and Malthus watched
it turn into a cesspool in real-time. Factories

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:39.139
dumped waste directly into the river, including
tannery chemicals and animal parts from slaughter

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:45.310
houses. Garbage was also just thrown in because
-- why not? It’ll just wash away -- and

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:51.210
it was the perfect outlet for human sewage.
Treating the Thames like a living dumpster

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:57.860
and infinite toilet killed wildlife, made
London stink, and fueled cholera outbreaks

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.350
that killed tens of thousands at a time.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:08.590
And it really STUNK -- July and August of
1858 is known as “The Great Stink,” and

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:14.419
Michael Faraday described The Thames as “feculence
rolled up in clouds so dense that they were

00:06:14.419 --> 00:06:16.680
visible at the surface.”

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:22.690
The point is that Malthus was witnessing progress
that even his grandparents couldn’t have

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:30.370
fathomed -- and he was also seeing misery,
social and biological putrefaction, and an

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:33.620
obviously unsustainable future.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:40.419
He concluded that the problem was people:
there were simply too many of them to feed.

00:06:40.419 --> 00:06:41.419
He wrote:

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:46.800
“It is an obvious truth, which has been
taken notice of by many writers, that population

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:53.250
must always be kept down to the level of the
means of subsistence.”

00:06:53.250 --> 00:07:00.150
To Malthus, the math is what made the threat
of overpopulation so obvious -- and the “Mathusian

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:06.629
catastrophe” came down to comparing exponential
growth to linear growth. Malthus argued that

00:07:06.629 --> 00:07:12.070
the human population left unchecked grows
exponentially, because… people like having

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:18.710
babies. In the 1700s, women of the lower class
tended to have about 8 children, with maybe

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:24.169
5 of them reaching adulthood. If 2 parents
produce 5 kids, and each of those produce

00:07:24.169 --> 00:07:32.250
5 more, and each of those produce 5 -- your
family grows from 2 to 7 to 32 to 157 in just

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:39.099
4 generations -- that's an increase of 7,750%
in the human footprint.

00:07:39.099 --> 00:07:45.230
And it was an economic problem, too: the more
people, the more available labor. The more

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:51.270
labor, the lower the wages. And the lower
the wages, the harder it is to get increasingly-expensive

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.180
food that everyone is competing over.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:08:00.340
Contrast that with an increase in food production
that is not exponential -- according to Malthus,

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:07.770
the available farmland can only produce so
much food. So, people must starve by the millions

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:14.970
to restore the balance. He wrote, “Subsistence
increases only in an arithmetical ratio,”

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:20.930
and that was that -- in the battle of geometric
vs. arithmetic progressions, the geometric

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.409
side is going to win.

00:08:23.409 --> 00:08:30.449
Malthus tried to depict “man as he really
is, inert, sluggish, and averse from labour,

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:38.150
unless compelled by necessity.” To him,
humanity was dumb and lazy -- especially the

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.289
poor.

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:45.240
As Malthus wrote the Principle of Population,
England was the pinnacle of modern civilization

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:50.709
-- but it was also in the wake of having suffered
through a costly Seven Years War, the loss

00:08:50.709 --> 00:08:56.649
of its American empire, and the day by day
threat of instability and conflict from Napoleon

00:08:56.649 --> 00:09:03.550
Bonaparte’s France. If everything that got
better also made things worse in the greatest

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:10.830
country in the world, why would he have an
optimistic outlook on the rest of humanity’s

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:11.830
future?

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:19.640
In 1971, Paul Ehrlich agreed. He said, "By
the year 2000 the United Kingdom will be simply

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:26.250
a small group of impoverished islands, inhabited
by some 70 million hungry people. I would

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:31.470
take even money that England will not exist
in the year 2000.”

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:37.519
In 1980, Julian Simon noticed that England
seemed to be doing just fine, and that what

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:43.990
Ehrlich saw as a set of problems destined
to threaten the fate of humanity were actually

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.160
an incredible opportunity.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:51.860
The really interesting thing about Julian
Simon is that he started out agreeing with

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:57.130
Ehrlich. In the 1960’s he published articles
arguing the importance of family planning

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:04.180
-- encouraging people to limit births -- on
economic grounds. His path to economics was…

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:10.279
circuitous. He studied experimental psychology
at Harvard, where he drove a cab, sold encyclopedias,

00:10:10.279 --> 00:10:15.970
and played poker to support himself. He also
worked in a tin can factory, a grass seed

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:23.019
factory, and a brewery, so he knew dirty work
and manual labor -- and he knew the problems

00:10:23.019 --> 00:10:24.470
with both.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:29.329
After college he enlisted in the Navy and
spent time in some of the less-glamorous working

00:10:29.329 --> 00:10:36.200
ports of the world where he saw the struggles
of poorer populations and the positive and

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:41.130
negative impacts of mechanization. He left
the Navy and took an interest in business

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:45.769
and economics, studying in a University of
Chicago department energized by the work of

00:10:45.769 --> 00:10:50.610
Milton Friedman and Friedrich Hayek. Through
the 60’s and 70’s he noticed that the

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:57.600
lives of the most vulnerable people in the
world were improving, and he did what virtually

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.100
no academic does now: changed his mind.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:07.089
Just like Malthus, it’s important to understand
the time in which Simon and Ehrlich kicked

00:11:07.089 --> 00:11:13.070
off their debate. When Ehrlich published The
Population Bomb in 1968, the world was one

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:19.220
generation removed from the most terrible
and destructive war it had ever seen -- total

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:25.970
military and civilian deaths from World War
2 and its effects were more than 80 million,

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:31.899
and the war ended with the debut of the most
terrifying weapon ever created. We’d spent

00:11:31.899 --> 00:11:37.920
the 20 years since worrying about which of
the two global superpowers would use it on

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:38.920
the other.

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:44.040
Meanwhile, in the United States, social conflict
drove riots in major cities, the last president

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:50.199
had been assassinated, and in ‘68 both Martin
Luther King Jr. and Bobby Kennedy were killed

00:11:50.199 --> 00:11:56.529
-- right as dissatisfaction with the Vietnam
War was peaking. Countries like China and

00:11:56.529 --> 00:12:03.110
India were struggling to feed billions. Ehrlich
was in the same situation as Malthus: sitting

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:08.209
in the country with the world’s highest
standard of living, what reasonable person

00:12:08.209 --> 00:12:14.850
would look at all that evidence and conclude
that, y'know what? Things are actually pretty

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:16.910
good!

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:21.610
Environmentalism had also branched out from
the simple conservation of the Teddy Roosevelt

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:27.430
era -- by the time Rachel Carson published
“Silent Spring” in 1962, which detailed

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:34.150
threats from pesticides used in agriculture
including DDT, a radical movement was already

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:39.290
happening. David Attenborough said that her
book changed the scientific world the most

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:45.480
behind only Darwin’s “Origin of Species”
-- and it opened up the floodgates not only

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:50.820
on exposing problems in the environment, but
rushing to fix them.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:56.650
Also in 1968, ecologist Garrett Hardin published
“The Tragedy of the Commons,” which tapped

00:12:56.650 --> 00:13:03.230
the metaphor of a public, unregulated plot
of land used rationally by individuals to

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:10.730
the point of total obliteration. Hardin wrote,
"Freedom in a commons brings ruin to all.”

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:17.460
-- and the message was that left to our own
devices, we were gonna destroy everything.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:24.610
Modifying human behavior is hard -- the simplest
solution was restricting the ability to make

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:33.240
humans at all, and if we didn’t do it ourselves,
nature would end up doing it for us.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:39.070
In the 1969 essay “Eco-Catastrophe,” Ehrlich
wrote that. “The shape of the population

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:44.590
growth curve is one familiar to biologists”
-- because like butterflies who interact with

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:51.230
and depend on their environment, populations
swell and shrink based on what that environment

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:58.240
can provide. He called it the “outbreak-crash
sequence” in which populations rise in times

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:05.750
of abundance and crash -- or completely disappear
-- when that abundance stops. A year later

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:11.290
he wrote in Audubon that “We’ve had most
of the outbreak -- what remains is mainly

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:17.970
the crash.” The world population at that
time was 3.7 billion and growing at a steady

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:23.850
2% per year, which put it on pace to reach
7 billion in 50 years.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:29.370
Malthus had already outlined two types of
interventions to defuse what would eventually

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:37.089
become Ehrlich’s Population Bomb: preventative
checks and positive checks. A preventative

00:14:37.089 --> 00:14:43.410
check is an act that controls population by
reducing fertility rates: deciding not to

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:47.730
have kids, limiting the number of children
parents can have -- like China’s former

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:54.580
one-child policy -- and the wider availability
of both contraception and abortion.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:15:01.750
A positive check is actually… not that positive.
It’s something that limits the human lifespan,

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:09.050
like war, disease, famine -- or murder. It
can be an active or passive de-populator.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:15.149
And you might be surprised at how many academics
were happy to mete out misery in the name

00:15:15.149 --> 00:15:16.220
of eco-justice.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:24.000
In their 1967 book “Famine! 1975,” brothers
William and Paul Paddock proposed that the

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:29.690
United States should limit its foreign food
aid based on the social behavior of countries

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:35.730
with growing populations. They categorized
Egypt and India as “can’t be saved.”

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:42.430
So, no food for them. Pakistan scored higher
-- the Paddocks thought they did deserve some

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:49.949
food. And the idea to withhold food in an
effort to encourage -- or really, punish -- behavior

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:56.029
was typified by President Lyndon Johnson’s
decision not to send food aid to India during

00:15:56.029 --> 00:16:03.200
the Bihar Famine of 1966. He told an advisor,
“I’m not going to piss away foreign aid

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:08.070
in nations where they refuse to deal with
their own population problems.”

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:12.740
Ehrlich supported food punishment. He said,
“I have been citing the Paddocks’ work

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:18.819
in all my recent talks on the population crisis,”
and he called food punishment “coercion

00:16:18.819 --> 00:16:24.329
for a good cause” because India was “hopeless.”

00:16:24.329 --> 00:16:29.589
It was India that actually inspired Ehrlich
to start thinking deeply about The Population

00:16:29.589 --> 00:16:36.279
Bomb. He had traveled to Delhi in 1965 with
his family, and what he saw was like a reboot

00:16:36.279 --> 00:16:38.280
of London in the time of Malthus:

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:43.800
“My wife and daughter and I were returning
to our hotel in an ancient taxi. The seats

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:49.380
were hopping with fleas. The only functional
gear was third. As we crawled through the

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:54.490
city, we entered a crowded slum area. The
temperature was well over 100, and the air

00:16:54.490 --> 00:17:01.360
was a haze of dust and smoke. The streets
seemed alive with people. People eating, people

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:06.200
watching, people sleeping. People visiting,
arguing, and screaming. People thrusting their

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:11.650
hands through the taxi window, begging. People
defecating and urinating. People clinging

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:18.120
to buses. People herding animals. People,
people, people, people. As we moved slowly

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:24.770
through the mob, hand horn squawking, the
dust, noise, heat, and cooking fires gave

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:28.189
the scene a hellish aspect.”

00:17:28.189 --> 00:17:33.720
Ehrlich knew something had to be done.

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:39.270
65 years ago Leonard Read detailed the seemingly
IMPOSSIBLE global cooperation that led to

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:44.580
the creation of a single pencil. Think about
everything involved in production: growing

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:49.289
and logging the cedar, mining the graphite,
truck drivers hauling it all and a guy in

00:17:49.289 --> 00:17:54.700
a lighthouse guiding shipping vessels into
port, it’s millions of people uniting on

00:17:54.700 --> 00:18:00.240
their own to make a simple little thing. And
he had no idea the Foundation for Economic

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:05.990
Education he created would turn his unique
perspective into YouTube videos.

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:11.450
Their Unscene series investigates the stories
behind film, television, and pop culture -- they

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:16.370
just released a video about how Disney caved
to Chinese pressure to suppress an incredible

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:22.260
Martin Scorcese film about the Dalai Lama.
And their animated Common Sense Soapbox channel

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:28.080
reveals how the world really works. You can
also check out Invisible Hands, a crash course

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:30.080
in economics hosted by… me.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:35.220
Thanks again to the Foundation for Economic
Education for supporting this video -- click

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:40.650
the link to their channel in the description
below. And if you can handle unique analysis

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:45.800
of complex systems of human behavior -- throw
them a sub.

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:49.980
Coercion wasn’t off the table for Ehrlich
and his colleagues, based on a tenet that

00:18:49.980 --> 00:18:55.340
parents did not have a natural right to have
children in the first place. He toyed with

00:18:55.340 --> 00:19:01.820
a worldwide limit of 2 children per set of
parents. Hardin agreed, saying, “The freedom

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:09.190
to breed is intolerable,” but warned that
“we ought not say anything foolish about

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:14.470
coercion” -- because trying to control people
by force would hurt the movement.

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:18.929
In The Population Bomb, Ehrlich said that
the government should take “whatever steps

00:19:18.929 --> 00:19:25.730
are necessary to establish a reasonable population
size in the United States,” with a “Department

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:32.880
of Population and Environment” that could
fund research into “mass sterilizing agents.”

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:39.350
Forced sterilization was an option for him
-- and so was introducing temporary infertility

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:46.131
agents in public drinking water. He said that
humanity “will wake up one day to find out

00:19:46.131 --> 00:19:52.049
that compulsory birth control offers the only
hope of survival.”

00:19:52.049 --> 00:19:58.290
Some of his allies became increasingly nervous
about the implications of Ehrlich’s more

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:04.669
radical ideas, including members of the group
he founded called Zero Population Growth -- which

00:20:04.669 --> 00:20:10.179
still exists, now called Population Connection
-- when they realized that the targets of

00:20:10.179 --> 00:20:19.360
their global sterilization efforts were not
going to be well-educated, well-off white

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:20.510
people.

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:27.390
But Ehrlich wasn’t the only one sounding
the alarm on a grand scale. 100 politicians,

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:33.340
diplomats, economists, and business leaders
had founded The Club of Rome in 1965, and

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:38.580
their 1972 “The Limits to Growth: A Report
for the Club of Rome’s Project on the Predicament

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:44.950
of Mankind” presented an MIT computer model
factoring in "population, food production,

00:20:44.950 --> 00:20:50.220
industrialization, pollution, and consumption
of nonrenewable natural resources."

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:57.351
They called for an absolute halt to economic
growth, warning that without it, industrial

00:20:57.351 --> 00:21:03.050
production would collapse, population would
‘suffer a catastrophic decline.’ and the

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:10.180
air, sea and land would be “polluted beyond
redemption.” And the right time to intervene

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:15.860
wasn’t today, it was actually yesterday,
because “the collapse will not come gradually,

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:21.039
but with awesome suddenness, with no way of
stopping it.”

00:21:21.039 --> 00:21:27.570
The movement’s most radical ideas became
reality in India as part of a forced sterilization

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:32.440
program in 1975. The BBC described the initiative:

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:38.460
“The drive to sterilise began in the 1970s
when, encouraged by loans amounting to tens

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:43.690
of millions of dollars from the World Bank,
the Swedish International Development Authority

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:49.600
and the UN Population Fund, India embarked
on an ambitious population control programme.

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:56.270
During the 1975 Emergency - when civil liberties
were suspended - Sanjay Gandhi, son of the

00:21:56.270 --> 00:22:02.289
former Prime Minister Indira Gandhi, began
what was described by many as a ‘gruesome

00:22:02.289 --> 00:22:09.990
campaign’ to sterilise poor men. There were
reports of police cordoning off villages and

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:12.700
virtually dragging the men to surgery.”

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:21.440
6.5 million Indian men were sterilized in
one year -- which was 15 times more than the

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:29.429
Nazis sterilized -- and about 2,000 of those
men died from surgical complications.

00:22:29.429 --> 00:22:33.890
In The Population Bomb’s chapter called
“What Needs to Be Done?” Ehrlich writes:

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:39.290
“When we suggested sterilizing all Indian
males with three or more children, he should

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:43.970
have encouraged the Indian government to go
ahead with the plan. We [the United States]

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:49.440
should have volunteered logistic support in
the form of helicopters, vehicles, and surgical

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:54.440
instruments. We should have sent doctors to
aid in the program by setting up centers for

00:22:54.440 --> 00:23:02.280
training paramedical personnel to do vasectomies.
Coercion? Perhaps, but coercion in a good

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:03.760
cause.”

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:11.450
His priority was to systematically eliminate
the world's fastest-growing populations -- which

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:16.650
were almost exclusively poor and non-white.

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:22.330
Even in his days of population concern, Julian
Simon had a softer approached than Ehrlich

00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:29.360
or The Club of Rome: he argued that avoiding
a birth cost a developing nation about $5,

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:35.320
and that it would save $114 -- which he called
“a fantastic bargain” -- and proposed

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:42.130
offering payments to families not to have
children as a mutually-agreeable preventative

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:50.120
check. His idea rewarded people for their
choices instead of sterilizing them against

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:51.150
their will.

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:58.340
But Simon eventually started to realize that
people didn’t work like butterflies.

00:23:58.340 --> 00:24:03.140
When he studied family dynamics and their
economic rationalism, he found that increased

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:08.940
income drove different behaviors among families
-- small families used additional money to

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:16.230
have larger families, and larger families
didn’t. Education levels had a marked effect

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:21.919
on reproduction, and still do. He looked to
works from Simon Kuznets and Richard Easterlin,

00:24:21.919 --> 00:24:29.440
who concluded that a larger population means
more creators and innovators, and Ester Boserup’s

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:35.950
work that showed population density determines
the type and scale of agricultural production

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:43.039
and its efficiency. Simon realized that population
growth was much more complex than Ehrlich

00:24:43.039 --> 00:24:50.080
made it out to be -- and not simply subject
to the conceptual Human Impact equation that

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:58.190
Ehrlich created, which was Impact = Population
x Affluence x Technology (I = P*A*T).

00:24:58.190 --> 00:25:06.000
But Ehrlich was the guy everyone wanted to
talk to. He was young, attractive, and highly

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:13.950
charismatic -- which made him totally unlike
most academics. He was really funny and likable

00:25:13.950 --> 00:25:19.549
as he detailed humanity’s impending doom
-- which is why he was a guest on The Tonight

00:25:19.549 --> 00:25:25.779
Show over twenty times. Here he is with Johnny
Carson talking about why he thinks the benefit

00:25:25.779 --> 00:25:29.110
of a growing population just… isn’t really
there:

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:32.630
Ehrlich: “Why don't we try doing a really
good job with 4 billion people? See if we

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:36.320
can do that. After we’ve got 4 billion people
well taken care of, in a clean environment

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:40.140
with good health, and everybody’s fed, and
everybody has opportunities and so on, then

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:42.832
we can say, well, gee, alright, maybe we can
do with 5… well, what would be the advantage

00:25:42.832 --> 00:25:46.260
of 5? Well, there would be another half a
billion women, for instance, which I would

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:47.260
find…”

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:48.260
Carson: “Well, you’d like that!”

00:25:48.260 --> 00:25:50.940
Ehrlich: “Yeah, right” [audience laughter]
“I’d just as soon have the 4 billion taken

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:52.149
care of right first.” [audience claps]

00:25:52.149 --> 00:25:55.529
Carson: “Yeah, I think you’re right. I
think so.”

00:25:55.529 --> 00:26:01.850
Ehrlich was masterful at presenting the science
of demographics and environmental impact in

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:10.320
moral and emotional terms. Do you want kids
to starve? Well… no. Do you want to perpetuate

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:17.500
human suffering? No. Do you want to see the
destruction of Earth, the human race, and

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:22.470
all plant and animal life? I don't know. PROBABLY
NOT.

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:27.500
And he fomented guilt, too -- especially among
high-consumption societies like the United

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:34.380
States and Western Europe. Is your standard
of living worth the price the rest of the

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:36.650
world pays?

00:26:36.650 --> 00:26:42.230
In “The Bet,” Paul Sabin writes that Ehrlich’s
brand of rhetoric played a significant part

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:50.120
in whipping up Neo-Malthusian fervor. He points
to a review of Ehrlich’s 1970 book “Population,

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:55.220
Resources, Environment: Issues in Human Ecology”
by Roger Revelle, who founded Harvard’s

00:26:55.220 --> 00:27:00.400
Center for Population Studies, that highlights
Ehrlich’s use of incendiary language:

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:05.929
“Staggering, sobering, disaster (three times),
enormously, drastically, catastrophic, dramatically,

00:27:05.929 --> 00:27:11.230
tremendous, highly lethal, extremely dangerous
(twice), especially virulent, more severe,

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:16.080
extremely fortunate, extremely vulnerable,
almost total, high potential, renewed spectre,

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:23.020
not gruesome enough, colossal hazard, biological
doomsday, superlethal, and disastrously effective”

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:28.570
Revelle noted what he called “apocalyptic
adverbs and adjectives” because that list

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:35.899
I just read appeared in only four pages out
of a book of over 300.

00:27:35.899 --> 00:27:43.570
Ehrlich had become a celebrity on TV, was
writing in Playboy -- and Julian Simon was

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:45.090
not happy.

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:51.940
The 70’s brought a series of energy crises
starting with the 1973 oil embargo that resulted

00:27:51.940 --> 00:27:58.220
in a 43% increase in the price of gas, gas
rationing in California based on license plate

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:05.549
numbers, and serious fears about dependence
on fossil fuels. So Ehrlich was looking pretty

00:28:05.549 --> 00:28:12.840
smart -- but Simon insisted that population
growth and its global problems should “thrill

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:15.039
rather than frighten us.”

00:28:15.039 --> 00:28:22.450
How!? How could he look at a world in crisis
and possibly think that?

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:27.060
Simon had spent the 70’s writing about all
the reasons the Ehrlich-style doomsdayers

00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:32.960
were wrong: wrong about their models, wrong
about humans, and really, just wrong about

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:36.260
the way the whole world works.

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:43.660
And he was writing, seemingly, to no one.
In a 1997 interview with Wired -- titled “The

00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:48.950
Doomslayer” -- he said seeing Ehrlich tout
such damaging theories “absolutely drove

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:55.260
me out of my skull… Here was a guy reaching
a vast audience, leading this juggernaut of

00:28:55.260 --> 00:29:03.350
environmentalist hysteria, and I felt utterly
helpless. What could I do? Go talk to five

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:04.350
people?"

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:08.990
Well, the truth of the matter is even those
5 people didn’t want to hear that between

00:29:08.990 --> 00:29:16.820
1800 and 1980, the price of wheat in the United
States dropped precipitously despite the population

00:29:16.820 --> 00:29:25.370
growing by about 4,500%. It didn’t matter
that Simon found that population did not actually

00:29:25.370 --> 00:29:31.270
increase geometrically, and birthrates had
even begun to fall in developed countries

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:36.790
-- including below “replacement rate,”
which is the minimum number of babies born

00:29:36.790 --> 00:29:44.669
to maintain current levels of population.
In 1950, global life expectancy was 45. By

00:29:44.669 --> 00:29:50.460
1980, it was 61 -- and that happened due to
better medicine, better technology, greater

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:59.300
food production, and greater wealth. That’s
about a 35% longer life in 30 years.

00:29:59.300 --> 00:30:06.419
All his frustration led to an article that
brought Simon and his optimism into the international

00:30:06.419 --> 00:30:13.350
debate over humanity’s future. In the June
27, 1980 issue of Science, Simon published

00:30:13.350 --> 00:30:20.390
“Resources, Population, Environment: An
Oversupply of False Bad News.” And the introductory

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:22.820
summary says it all:

00:30:22.820 --> 00:30:28.710
“False bad news about population growth,
natural resources, and the environment is

00:30:28.710 --> 00:30:35.480
published widely in the face of contradictory
evidence. For example, the world supply of

00:30:35.480 --> 00:30:41.110
arable land has actually been increasing,
the scarcity of natural resources including

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:47.450
food and energy has been decreasing, and basic
measures of U.S. environmental quality show

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:53.990
positive trends. The aggregate data show no
long-run negative effect of population growth

00:30:53.990 --> 00:30:59.840
upon standard of living. Models that embody
forces omitted in the past, especially the

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:07.110
influence of population size upon productivity
increase, suggest a long-run positive effect

00:31:07.110 --> 00:31:09.730
of additional people.”

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:15.909
Simon completely decimated claims that arable
land had decreased worldwide -- instead, he

00:31:15.909 --> 00:31:22.590
noted that cultivated land was rising at 1%
per year. He showed that farmers in industrialized

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:29.279
nations were producing more food on less land
-- as populations grew, people were getting

00:31:29.279 --> 00:31:35.630
better at farming and technology was making
it cheaper and easier with fewer people involved.

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:41.250
To the charge that the world increasingly
struggled with food, Simon showed that per

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:47.289
capita food production had also grown at about
1% per year.

00:31:47.289 --> 00:31:53.779
Now It wasn’t good news, yay yay yay, cotton
candy and sunshine, all around, and it never

00:31:53.779 --> 00:31:58.580
will be. Simon was honest about that. But
he made the distinction that the food problems

00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:04.480
in vulnerable countries were overwhelmingly
the result of restricted markets, war, and

00:32:04.480 --> 00:32:09.039
political instability. He called out The New
York Times, the United Nations, the World

00:32:09.039 --> 00:32:15.050
Bank -- and, of course, Ehrlich. And he lamented
the reliance on faulty computer models that

00:32:15.050 --> 00:32:21.950
delivered grim, and ultimately wrong, projections
about our future. He concluded that:

00:32:21.950 --> 00:32:27.880
“Population growth and productivity increase
are not independent forces running a race.

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:34.490
Rather, additional persons cause technological
advances by inventing, adapting, and diffusing

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:36.470
new productive knowledge.”

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:41.769
He also wasn’t the first to challenge Malthus
and Ehrlich on these grounds -- and he had

00:32:41.769 --> 00:32:45.669
some unexpected allies throughout history.

00:32:45.669 --> 00:32:52.350
Karl Marx called Malthus a “plagiarist,”
a “sycophant,” and a “reverend scribbler,”

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:57.840
and Engels refuted Malthus’s concept of
nature’s inevitable processes, writing that,

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:02.780
“... the determining factor in history…
is the production and reproduction of the

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:09.040
immediate essentials of life.” Marx and
Engels saw the solution in a tightly-controlled

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:14.919
collectivism that would eventually become
Communism, while Simon pointed to the power

00:33:14.919 --> 00:33:22.580
of freedom and markets -- but they all believed
that the way we organize society can address

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:28.080
population problems that seem hopeless. But
aren't.

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:32.970
Economist Henry George stated the difference
most simply in his 1879 “Progress and Poverty,”

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:39.179
saying, "Both the jayhawk and the man eat
chickens; but the more jayhawks, the fewer

00:33:39.179 --> 00:33:44.179
chickens, while the more men, the more chickens."

00:33:44.179 --> 00:33:48.740
Monarch butterflies eat nectar produced from
flowering plants. If there’s not enough

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:54.929
nectar to support the monarch butterfly population,
some of them die until it reaches a sustainable

00:33:54.929 --> 00:34:01.130
level. But if humans have restricted access
to a particular resource -- like, corn or

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:07.700
something -- or it becomes too expensive,
we can just use something else. Simon argued

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:14.750
that humanity has two superpowers: adaptability
and innovation. We can invent, we can improve,

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:22.040
and we can substitute, all of which constitutes
less of a threat to resources than Ehrlich

00:34:22.040 --> 00:34:23.040
thought.

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:28.970
Because, to Simon, those finite resources
aren’t nearly as finite as they seem. He

00:34:28.970 --> 00:34:34.889
used copper as an example, saying that we
can’t calculate future quantities of copper

00:34:34.889 --> 00:34:40.860
because: “new lodes, new methods of mining
copper, and variations in grades of copper

00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:45.179
lodes; because copper can be made from other
metals; and because of the vagueness of the

00:34:45.179 --> 00:34:50.659
boundaries within which copper might be found
– including the sea, and other planets.

00:34:50.659 --> 00:34:56.010
Even less possible is a reasonable calculation
of the amount of future services of the sort

00:34:56.010 --> 00:35:01.000
we are now accustomed to get from copper,
because of recycling and because of the substitution

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:05.840
of other materials for copper, as in the case
of the communications satellite.”

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:11.930
Translation, we are good at finding more,
we’re getting more efficient at extracting

00:35:11.930 --> 00:35:17.930
it, we’re using it more smartly, and how
we use it now doesn’t have any bearing on

00:35:17.930 --> 00:35:22.600
how we’ll use it in the future -- if we
use it at all.

00:35:22.600 --> 00:35:27.580
Simon wrote in Science that we have so much
bad news about the world because that’s

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:33.240
where the funding is for researchers -- and
that late night talk shows don’t have guests

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:38.501
who talk about everything getting better.
But he also said that the “cumulative nature

00:35:38.501 --> 00:35:45.250
of exponential growth models” have the “power
to seduce and bewitch,” especially because

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:52.859
so many people compare the present with the
potential future -- idealized or awful -- instead

00:35:52.859 --> 00:35:56.190
of comparing the present to the past.

00:35:56.190 --> 00:36:01.980
Simon and his growing ranks of optimists were
derided as adhering to “cornucopianism”

00:36:01.980 --> 00:36:05.970
-- referencing the Greek mythological horn
of abundance that produced food and never

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:11.740
ran out. Ehrlich said Simon was “like the
guy who jumps off the Empire State Building

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:16.230
and says how great things are going so far
as he passes the 10th floor.”

00:36:16.230 --> 00:36:22.589
Ehrlich insisted that not only did we have
finite resources, but that we were using them

00:36:22.589 --> 00:36:29.800
up fast, while Simon was effectively arguing
that resources were endless -- and it didn’t

00:36:29.800 --> 00:36:35.540
help that he wrote that humanity’s only
real limit was the theoretical total weight

00:36:35.540 --> 00:36:42.870
of the universe. To Ehrlich, we were doomed;
to Simon, humans not only had the capacity

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:49.880
to address our problems, but that we’d come
out even better than before we had them. It

00:36:49.880 --> 00:36:52.650
was kind of a big difference.

00:36:52.650 --> 00:36:56.980
So the battle lines had finally been drawn
-- and it was time to make a wager on the

00:36:56.980 --> 00:37:01.760
future of the human race. It was time for
the bet.

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:06.640
In Social Science Quarterly, Simon said he’d
like to take that bet with Ehrlich about England

00:37:06.640 --> 00:37:13.130
still existing 20 years on -- but instead,
he proposed "to stake $10,000 ... on my belief

00:37:13.130 --> 00:37:18.350
that the cost of non-government-controlled
raw materials (including grain and oil) will

00:37:18.350 --> 00:37:21.280
not rise in the long run."

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:26.630
Simon said that Ehrlich could choose any 5
raw materials he wanted -- Simon left it up

00:37:26.630 --> 00:37:33.400
to him -- and that they would track the prices
of each commodity over the course of 10 years.

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:41.130
On September 29, 1980, Ehrlich agreed to a
reduced wager of $1,000, putting $200 into

00:37:41.130 --> 00:37:47.240
each of 5 resources he and his colleagues
had predicted would rise in price over the

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:53.839
coming decade: copper, chromium, nickel, tin,
and tungsten. If the inflation-adjusted prices

00:37:53.839 --> 00:38:00.120
went up, Simon would pay Ehrlich their difference
in values between 1980 and 1990. If their

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:05.040
prices went down, Simon would win the bet
and Ehrlich would pay the difference.

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:10.430
Copper has been used in various capacities
for over 10,000 years, from primitive tools

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:15.440
to modern conductors. Chromium is a component
to stainless steel, and it’s why your dad’s

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:20.411
wrenches are hard and don’t rust. Nickel
is one of four ferromagnetic elements; we

00:38:20.411 --> 00:38:25.380
use it now in the rechargeable batteries of
electric vehicles. Tin is important for alloys

00:38:25.380 --> 00:38:31.650
and plating -- which is why plated steel cans
were referred to as “tin cans.” Tungsten

00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:38.490
is extremely hard and dense, and it’s necessary
in x-ray tubes and welding. These 5 materials

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:45.000
presented a cross-section of nature-dependent
human activity that Ehrlich was convinced

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:48.540
would force demand and scarcity to collide.

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:54.260
The 1980s saw a dramatic policy shift in the
United States under Ronald Reagan, who advocated

00:38:54.260 --> 00:39:00.700
for a freer market in line with Simon’s
optimism. Fears about endangered species became

00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:06.079
real, holes in the Ozone layer threatened
to increase exposure to ultraviolet radiation,

00:39:06.079 --> 00:39:11.380
and the Exxon Valdez oil spill dominated the
news at the end of the decade. The threat

00:39:11.380 --> 00:39:18.410
of nuclear war still loomed, and a nuclear
disaster in Chernobyl came 7 years after Three

00:39:18.410 --> 00:39:23.380
Mile Island. So things did not seem awesome.

00:39:23.380 --> 00:39:28.310
But on September 29, 1990, it was time for
someone to pay up.

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:37.920
In 1980, the price of chromium was $3.90 per
pound. In 1990, it was $3.70. Tin dropped

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:48.869
by half from $8.72 to $3.88. Three of the
five metals had gone down in price despite

00:39:48.869 --> 00:39:54.280
inflation, and the other two both fell in
inflation-adjusted terms.

00:39:54.280 --> 00:40:02.470
Simon won the bets on all 5 of the chosen
metals -- but more importantly to him, Ehrlich

00:40:02.470 --> 00:40:05.050
and his doomsday philosophy lost.

00:40:05.050 --> 00:40:15.030
In October of 1990, a check from Wells Fargo
Bank in the amount of $576.07 arrived in Julian

00:40:15.030 --> 00:40:21.730
Simon’s mailbox. There was no note, and
the check was signed by Anne Ehrlich -- Paul’s

00:40:21.730 --> 00:40:22.730
wife.

00:40:22.730 --> 00:40:26.640
In his book “The Ultimate Resource,” Julian
Simon wrote:

00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:34.579
“Adding more people causes problems. But
people are also the means to solve these problems.

00:40:34.579 --> 00:40:40.040
The main fuel to speed the world’s progress
is our stock of knowledge; the brakes are

00:40:40.040 --> 00:40:46.180
our lack of imagination and unsound social
regulations of these activities. The ultimate

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:53.750
resource is people—especially skilled, spirited,
and hopeful young people endowed with liberty—who

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:59.210
will exert their wills and imaginations for
their own benefits, and so inevitably they

00:40:59.210 --> 00:41:02.370
will benefit the rest of us as well.”

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:11.240
It was truly a triumph of hope and optimism
over doom and gloom -- or did Simon just get

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:12.240
lucky?

00:41:12.240 --> 00:41:17.820
A 2009 paper called “Luck or Skill? An Examination
of the Ehrlich-Simon Bet” reveals that had

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:24.700
the bet been run one more time from 1990-2000,
Simon would’ve triumphed again -- but had

00:41:24.700 --> 00:41:31.980
the wager taken place for any of the possible
98 ten-year periods between 1900 and 2007,

00:41:31.980 --> 00:41:41.450
Ehrlich would’ve won 61.2% of the time.
So maybe Ehrlich was right? Naaaaaah.

00:41:41.450 --> 00:41:49.589
Actually, the data shows a massive crash in
commodities prices following World War I -- which

00:41:49.589 --> 00:41:55.700
means they spent the rest of the century slowly
rising back to their former levels, creating

00:41:55.700 --> 00:42:01.609
an artificial bias toward Ehrlich’s rising
prices theory and proving Simon’s exact

00:42:01.609 --> 00:42:07.220
point about the power of human cooperation
-- or in the case of a World War, the lack

00:42:07.220 --> 00:42:12.349
of cooperation -- to determine how we use
and value resources.

00:42:12.349 --> 00:42:17.989
It’s easy to look at the results of the
Ehrlich-Simon wager and scoff at the loser

00:42:17.989 --> 00:42:24.510
-- and it’s even easier to revisit failed
doomsday predictions of a disappearing England

00:42:24.510 --> 00:42:32.530
and worldwide famine killing billions and
laugh at how wrong it all was. It's much harder

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:39.690
to understand why those predictions happen
in the first place -- and why it's completely

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:48.880
natural to think everything is bad and getting
worse. It's why the doomsayers are so seductive

00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:56.210
and popular, and why the doomslayers aren't
just ignored -- they're hated.

00:42:56.210 --> 00:43:02.330
In 2016, Swedish physician Hans Rosling was
diagnosed with pancreatic cancer. He began

00:43:02.330 --> 00:43:07.099
writing “Factfulness: Ten Reasons We’re
Wrong About the World,” as what he called

00:43:07.099 --> 00:43:13.590
his “last battle in my life-long mission
to fight devastating ignorance” -- it was

00:43:13.590 --> 00:43:21.770
published in 2018 a year after he died. Rosling
detailed 10 human instincts that conspire

00:43:21.770 --> 00:43:29.390
to convince us that the world is forever going
in the wrong direction -- which can inform

00:43:29.390 --> 00:43:35.349
devastatingly-wrong reactions to our problems.
The first is the “Gap Instinct,” which

00:43:35.349 --> 00:43:41.080
is to consider the differences between the
top and the bottom rather than focusing on

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:47.490
the majority or whether all levels of society
are improving in spite of relative differences.

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:55.540
And he argues that our “Negative Instinct”
programs us not to bother noticing improvements

00:43:55.540 --> 00:43:56.810
at all.

00:43:56.810 --> 00:44:02.030
Our “Straight Line Instinct” causes us
to assume that our current trends will continue

00:44:02.030 --> 00:44:08.890
exactly as they are right now rather than
accelerating or decelerating, and our “Fear

00:44:08.890 --> 00:44:16.480
Instinct” distorts our perception of risk
to make us overvalue possible negatives.

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:24.080
Our “Size Instinct” makes us feel overwhelmed
by the sheer scale of a problem as our “Generalization

00:44:24.080 --> 00:44:31.319
Instinct” lumps all our problems together
as being equally-threatening -- and our “Destiny

00:44:31.319 --> 00:44:37.530
Instinct” makes us feel like none of our
problems ever get solved even though they

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:40.820
tend to get better slowly.

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:47.150
Our “Single-Perspective Instinct” hyper-focuses
on just one possible solution rather than

00:44:47.150 --> 00:44:53.559
considering many solutions that work in combination,
and our “Blame Instinct” makes us focus

00:44:53.559 --> 00:45:00.240
on the bad guys who we’re convinced are
responsible for the problem. But the most

00:45:00.240 --> 00:45:06.520
threatening might be the “Urgency Instinct,”
which drives us to feel that there's an impending

00:45:06.520 --> 00:45:14.040
catastrophe if we don’t do something immediately
rather than one step at a time.

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:20.880
Only a single comma separates “Do something,
stupid!” from “Do something stupid.”

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:28.610
Ehrlich was 0 for 5 on his bet with Julian
Simon, but he’s 10 for 10 on Rosling’s

00:45:28.610 --> 00:45:32.890
checklist of instinctual pathologies.

00:45:32.890 --> 00:45:40.020
Paul Ehrlich is now in his 90’s, and he’s
vibrant, energetic, and still insisting that

00:45:40.020 --> 00:45:46.730
he’s right -- or that he was never that
wrong. In January of this year, Ehrlich tweeted

00:45:46.730 --> 00:45:52.819
after being featured on 60 Minutes that, “If
I'm always wrong so is science, since my work

00:45:52.819 --> 00:45:59.510
is always peer-reviewed, including the POPULATION
BOMB and I've gotten virtually every scientific

00:45:59.510 --> 00:46:04.050
honor. Sure I've made some mistakes, but no
basic ones.”

00:46:04.050 --> 00:46:10.770
Elon Musk disagrees. In June, he said that,
“Paul Ehrlich has done immense damage to

00:46:10.770 --> 00:46:14.470
humanity. Immense. I despise him.”

00:46:14.470 --> 00:46:19.230
In April of 2023, Ehrlich gave an interview
at Stanford in which he said that he wouldn’t

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:22.619
deny anyone the opportunity to have children,
but that:

00:46:22.619 --> 00:46:28.660
“... if you have 10 children, you’re an
evil person trying to basically destroy the

00:46:28.660 --> 00:46:29.660
world.”

00:46:29.660 --> 00:46:36.661
The birth rate in Somalia is 7.5 times higher
than in South Korea -- but it isn’t clear

00:46:36.661 --> 00:46:42.710
whether Ehrlich thinks Somalians are 7.5 times
more evil.

00:46:42.710 --> 00:46:48.869
In 1981, the year after the Ehrlich-Simon
wager -- and the year in which Simon’s “The

00:46:48.869 --> 00:46:55.390
Ultimate Resource" came out -- 88% of the
population of China lived in extreme poverty.

00:46:55.390 --> 00:47:01.450
Deng Xioping’s market reforms then fueled
tremendous economic growth and an increased

00:47:01.450 --> 00:47:09.559
standard of living. China’s extreme poverty
rate today is under 1%, there are no longer

00:47:09.559 --> 00:47:16.420
any restrictions on reproduction, and China
has more billionaires than any other country

00:47:16.420 --> 00:47:17.420
in the world.

00:47:17.420 --> 00:47:27.859
In 1980, the average daily caloric intake
globally was 2,487; today it’s 2,928. In

00:47:27.859 --> 00:47:38.260
India, it rose from 1,994 50 years ago to
2,533 today -- and in a long list of countries,

00:47:38.260 --> 00:47:43.190
we now have a new problem of consuming too
many calories.

00:47:43.190 --> 00:47:51.250
Life expectancy globally was just 61 years
in 1980; today it’s about 73.

00:47:51.250 --> 00:48:01.810
In 2022, about 630,000 people worldwide died
from AIDS-related illnesses -- down 69% from

00:48:01.810 --> 00:48:08.700
about 2 million per year just 20 years ago,
and you'll now find TV commercials for competing

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:15.359
commercials for combination antiretroviral
drugs that reduce the HIV virus to undetectable

00:48:15.359 --> 00:48:18.290
levels.

00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:24.200
In 1998, Julian Simon was jumping rope on
his deck. The phone rang, and his wife went

00:48:24.200 --> 00:48:29.630
upstairs to answer it. It was their daughter
Judith with news that she was pregnant with

00:48:29.630 --> 00:48:32.150
Julian’s first grandchild.

00:48:32.150 --> 00:48:39.020
A grandchild that was not just one more mouth
to feed, but another human with potentially

00:48:39.020 --> 00:48:46.329
unlimited creative capacity -- a brand new
human mind added on to the 6 billion of the

00:48:46.329 --> 00:48:54.829
time, one with the power to innovate, to identify
new problems and to solve the old ones -- or

00:48:54.829 --> 00:48:59.329
maybe just one more human to make life worth
living.

00:48:59.329 --> 00:49:06.250
Not an insect whose primitive cerebral ganglia
draws it into a flame to its own death — but

00:49:06.250 --> 00:49:13.400
a person who can harness that flame to cook
food, heat their houses, and smelt ore from

00:49:13.400 --> 00:49:22.210
the rocks they dig up. Julian Simon’s grandchild
would be one more soul in a world he knew

00:49:22.210 --> 00:49:30.170
got a little better every day, even when it
didn’t feel that way.

00:49:30.170 --> 00:49:37.200
Julian’s wife ran down the stairs to give
him the great news. She found him dead on

00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:42.819
the living room floor. He’d had a heart
attack at 65.

00:49:42.819 --> 00:49:51.980
Paul Ehrlich’s Population Bomb theory was
right -- about butterflies. Since his 1980

00:49:51.980 --> 00:49:59.960
bet with Simon, the Western Monarch Butterfly
has seen a population reduction of over 95%.

00:49:59.960 --> 00:50:05.950
We cleared forests for farming, and the milkweed
that Monarchs feed on flourished. Herbicides

00:50:05.950 --> 00:50:14.240
to control weeds in crops shrunk available
milkweed, and butterfly populations were devastated.

00:50:14.240 --> 00:50:21.470
But we’ve responded by doing exactly what
Simon said we would: creating a rehabilitation

00:50:21.470 --> 00:50:32.230
plan that includes planting 1.3 billion milkweed
stems through the Central Migratory Flyway.

00:50:32.230 --> 00:50:39.900
Everyone knows we are mired in problems and
the consequences of our progress — but the

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:48.870
one way to avoid armageddon is to think beyond
the doom, to resist the siren song of our

00:50:48.870 --> 00:51:01.780
most basic instincts, and to make use of our
one ultimate finite resource… you.

00:51:01.780 --> 00:51:14.020
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:51:14.020 --> 00:51:21.460
"Because it has been agreed by the nations
of the world that the Earth can no longer

00:51:21.460 --> 00:51:27.940
sustain a continuously increasing population,
child-bearing is herewith forbidden."

